# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — guide for coding agents

This is a Groundhog smart contract: a `no_std` Rust library compiled to
RV32 and deployed on-chain. Parts of this scaffold's shape are contracts
with the Groundhog toolchain, not style choices. This file states them so
they survive refactors. Do not "clean them up".

## Build and test with the groundhog CLI — never bare cargo for RV32

- `groundhog build` — compiles the deployable artifact to
  `target/groundhog/{{PROJECT_NAME_SNAKE}}.rv32a`.
- Always run BOTH test commands before calling a change done — they cover
  different things and neither replaces the other:
  - `cargo test` — the unit tests (`tests/test.rs`), compiled for the host
    and run against the in-process mock executor.
  - `groundhog test` — the end-to-end tests (`tests/test_rv32.rs`, with
    the `dev` feature): builds the deployable RV32 artifact and executes
    it in the real RV32 executor. A passing unit test proves nothing
    about the artifact — for one, release builds wrap on integer overflow
    where host builds panic.
- `groundhog test --integration` — RPC tests against an ephemeral local node.
- A bare `cargo build --target riscv32im-unknown-none-elf` can never work:
  the required `build-std` flag lives in `.cargo/contract.toml`, which only
  `groundhog build` passes to cargo. Errors about duplicate lang items or
  panic strategies mean "use `groundhog build`", not that the project is
  broken.

## Names the toolchain keys on — do not rename

- Feature `entrypoints`: the `#[contract]` macro emits RV32 entrypoints
  only under `cfg(all(target_arch = "riscv32", feature = "entrypoints"))`,
  and `groundhog build` compiles the bin with exactly this feature.
- Feature `dev`: `groundhog test` (and its test targets) run with the
  `dev` feature enabled.
- Feature `groundhog-integration-test`: enabled by the CLI for
  `groundhog test --integration`; `build.rs` activates the separate
  runner cfg.
- The `[[bin]]` name must stay the snake_case package name
  (`{{PROJECT_NAME_SNAKE}}`): `groundhog build` derives `--bin` from the
  package name and reads the ELF it produced under that name.

## Manifest rules

- Never make `dev` a default feature. A crate that depends on this one
  would drag `sdk/dev` (std) into RV32 guest builds through cargo feature
  unification, and the build fails far from the mistake.
- The self-referential dev-dependency
  (`{{PROJECT_NAME}} = { path = ".", features = ["dev"] }`) is deliberate:
  it enables `dev` for `cargo test` builds only, keeping the library
  `no_std` for guest builds. Do not simplify it away.
- If another contract crate depends on this one, forward `dev` through the
  dependent's own feature: `dev = ["sdk/dev", "{{PROJECT_NAME}}/dev"]`.
- Keep `sdk` pinned to a version published in the Groundhog registry; the
  scaffold's pin is managed by the groundhog CLI.

## Files that must stay at the directory `groundhog build` runs from

Cargo reads its config only from the invocation directory upward, and the
groundhog CLI discovers `groundhog.toml` the same way starting at the
cargo workspace root, so these must sit at the workspace root:

- `.cargo/config.toml` — RV32 target rustflags, including the linker script.
- `.cargo/contract.toml` — the `build-std` overlay `groundhog build` passes.
- `rust-toolchain.toml` — pinned nightly plus the RV32 target.
- `groundhog.toml` — profiles; a copy left behind in a member directory is
  never found, and `groundhog build` fails with "no active profile".

If this project is moved into a parent cargo workspace, move all four to
the workspace root. Never copy the `[target.*]` rustflags into a second
config file on the same discovery chain: cargo joins target rustflags
across layers, and a duplicated `-Tgroundhog-rv32.ld` fails the link with
"region 'CODE' already defined".

## Layout

- `src/implementation.rs` — all contract logic: state struct, errors, the
  `#[contract]` impl.
- `src/lib.rs` — `no_std` gate, `sdk::declare_mock_contract!();`, and
  re-exports. Keep it minimal.
- `src/main.rs` — the RV32 bin wrapper. Do not add code here.
- `tests/test.rs` — native tests against the in-process mock executor.
- `tests/test_rv32.rs` — RV32-executor tests, run via `groundhog test`.
- `tests/integration.rs` — RPC tests for `groundhog test --integration`.
